    <!-- TAB 1: THE STORY -->
    <div class="tab-content">
        <div class="card">
            <h2>The story, in three parts</h2>
            <p>Read each part. After it, there's a quick comprehension check (try the questions from memory first), then one thing to discuss.</p>

            <h3>Part 1 — How the roads opened</h3>
            <div class="story-chunk">
                <p>There was never a single "Silk Road." The name was invented in the 1870s by a German geographer, Ferdinand von Richthofen, to describe something far messier and grander: a shifting web of overland and sea routes that, for well over a thousand years, connected China to Central Asia, India, Persia, Arabia and the shores of the Mediterranean. No merchant walked its whole length. Goods were carried in stages, passed from one set of traders to the next, so that a bolt of cloth might change hands a dozen times before it reached Rome.</p>
                <p>The overland network is usually dated to around 130 BCE. The Han emperor of China sent an envoy, Zhang Qian, westward to seek allies against nomadic enemies. He was captured and held for years, but he came home with something more valuable than an alliance: detailed knowledge of the rich civilisations that lay to the west. Trade quickly followed the path his reports had opened. Chinese silk was prized in the Roman world, where the secret of how it was made was completely unknown; for a long time the Chinese held a strict monopoly, and the mystery only added to the cloth's astonishing value.</p>
                <p>The desert stretches were brutal, above all the Taklamakan, whose name was said to warn travellers that those who went in did not come out. Survival depended on oasis towns strung across the sands, where caravans of camels were watered and rested. These places grew rich simply by sitting on the route, taxing and trading with everyone who passed.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="check">
                <div class="q">Quick check — answer from memory, then reveal:</div>
                <p>1. Why is "the Silk Road" a slightly misleading name?</p>
                <p>2. Who was Zhang Qian, and what did his journey lead to?</p>
                <button class="reveal-btn" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('show')">Reveal answers</button>
                <div class="answers">
                    <p><strong>1.</strong> It was not one road but a shifting network of many overland and sea routes; the name was coined much later, in the 1870s, by a German geographer.</p>
                    <p><strong>2.</strong> A Han envoy sent west around 130 BCE; captured and held for years, he returned with knowledge of western civilisations, and trade followed the routes he had mapped.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="discuss">
                <strong>Discuss:</strong>
                <ul><li>The Chinese kept the secret of silk for centuries. Is it ever right for one group to guard valuable knowledge, or should useful discoveries be shared?</li></ul>
            </div>

            <h3>Part 2 — What actually travelled</h3>
            <div class="story-chunk">
                <p>Silk gave the network its later name, but it was only one commodity among hundreds. Westward flowed silk, porcelain, tea, paper and spices; eastward came gold, silver, glass, wool, horses and, crucially, ideas. The most powerful cargo was often invisible. Paper-making and, much later, printing spread west out of China. Gunpowder and the compass followed the same routes. In the other direction, mathematics, astronomy and glass-making techniques travelled east, while artistic styles blended into entirely new forms wherever cultures met.</p>
                <p>Religion moved with the merchants. Buddhism was carried from India along the roads into China, Korea and Japan, and vast cliff-side cave temples such as Dunhuang were cut and painted where the faith took root. Later, Islam spread along the same channels, as did Christianity in its eastern forms and the Persian faith of Zoroastrianism. The great hub cities — Samarkand, Bukhara, Kashgar, Merv — became dazzling centres of learning where languages, beliefs and goods were exchanged side by side.</p>
                <p>This exchange was made possible by empires that could keep order over huge distances. When a single power held long stretches of the route, the roads were safer and trade flourished. The most striking example came in the thirteenth century, when the Mongol Empire united much of Asia under one rule. This so-called "Pax Mongolica" made travel remarkably secure, and it was in this window that a Venetian named Marco Polo made his famous journey east to the court of Kublai Khan.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="check">
                <div class="q">Quick check — answer from memory, then reveal:</div>
                <p>1. Beyond silk, name two goods and two "invisible" things that travelled the routes.</p>
                <p>2. What was the "Pax Mongolica," and why did it matter for trade?</p>
                <button class="reveal-btn" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('show')">Reveal answers</button>
                <div class="answers">
                    <p><strong>1.</strong> Goods: any two of porcelain, tea, paper, spices, glass, horses, gold, silver. Invisible: technologies (paper, printing, gunpowder, the compass), religions (Buddhism, Islam), mathematics, art and language.</p>
                    <p><strong>2.</strong> A period when the Mongol Empire united much of Asia under one rule, making the routes far safer; trade and travel flourished, and this is when Marco Polo travelled east.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="discuss">
                <strong>Discuss:</strong>
                <ul><li>Technologies, religions and art all moved along these routes. Which do you think changed the world more — the goods that were traded, or the ideas that travelled with them?</li></ul>
            </div>

            <h3>Part 3 — Decline, and the long echoes</h3>
            <div class="story-chunk">
                <p>The same roads that carried silk and scripture also carried death. In the fourteenth century a plague — almost certainly the disease remembered in Europe as the Black Death — is thought to have moved along the trade lines out of Central Asia, carried in the fleas of rats that travelled with the caravans and ships. It killed tens of millions across Asia, the Middle East and Europe, and the shock helped to break the very networks along which it had spread.</p>
                <p>Other forces pulled the overland routes apart. The vast Mongol Empire fragmented, and the security it had provided vanished. Then, from the fifteenth century, Europeans began to find sea routes to Asia around Africa, and later across the oceans. Ships could carry far more cargo, far more cheaply, than any caravan of camels. Slowly the great desert trade withered, and the oasis cities that had grown fat on it faded into quiet ruins.</p>
                <p>Yet the echoes never stopped sounding. The technologies that had travelled west — paper, printing, gunpowder, the compass — helped set Europe's later transformation in motion. Faiths that had spread along the roads still shape the map of belief today. The very idea of a connected world of trade, in which distant goods and ideas are exchanged across continents, was rehearsed on these routes for over a millennium. In our own century the name has been consciously revived, as China's vast "Belt and Road" projects reach again for the old dream of a network binding East and West.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="check">
                <div class="q">Quick check — answer from memory, then reveal:</div>
                <p>1. Give two reasons the overland Silk Road declined.</p>
                <p>2. Name two "long echoes" the Silk Road left on the modern world.</p>
                <button class="reveal-btn" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('show')">Reveal answers</button>
                <div class="answers">
                    <p><strong>1.</strong> Any two of: the plague (Black Death) that spread along the routes and shattered them; the break-up of the Mongol Empire and loss of security; and the rise of cheaper European sea routes to Asia.</p>
                    <p><strong>2.</strong> Any two of: technologies (paper, printing, gunpowder, the compass) that reshaped Europe; the spread of world religions; the idea of connected global trade; and its modern revival in China's "Belt and Road" projects.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="discuss">
                <strong>Discuss:</strong>
                <ul><li>The same network carried both extraordinary wealth and a devastating plague. Does the story of the Silk Road show that greater connection between peoples is worth its risks?</li></ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- TAB 2: GRAMMAR -->
    <div class="tab-content">
        <div class="card">
            <h2>Grammar — the passive voice</h2>
            <p>The Silk Road is a story of things being done, but often we don't know or don't care exactly <em>who</em> did them. Silk <em>was traded</em>; ideas <em>were carried</em>; a plague <em>was spread</em>. English handles this with the passive voice — and it's the natural way to describe processes, history and change when the action matters more than the actor.</p>
            <div class="note">
                <strong>How the passive is built:</strong><br>
                <strong>the object of the action + the right tense of <em>be</em> + the past participle.</strong><br>
                • Present simple: "Silk <em>is woven</em> from the thread of silkworms."<br>
                • Past simple: "Paper-making <em>was carried</em> westward from China."<br>
                • Present perfect: "The old routes <em>have been revived</em> in recent years."<br>
                • With a modal: "The desert <em>could not be crossed</em> without water."
            </div>
            <div class="note">
                <strong>When we choose the passive:</strong><br>
                • when the doer is <strong>unknown or unimportant</strong>: "Goods <em>were passed</em> from hand to hand."<br>
                • when we want to <strong>focus on the thing affected</strong>: "Buddhism <em>was carried</em> along the roads."<br>
                • If we still want to name the doer, we add <strong>by</strong>: "The name <em>was coined</em> <strong>by</strong> a German geographer."
            </div>

            <h3>1. Make it passive</h3>
            <p>Rewrite each active sentence in the passive. Keep the same tense.</p>
            <div class="gap-fill">
                <p>1. Merchants carried silk to Rome. → Silk <input type="text" placeholder="..."> to Rome.</p>
                <p>2. The Chinese guarded the secret for centuries. → The secret <input type="text" placeholder="..."> for centuries.</p>
                <p>3. Traders have revived the old routes. → The old routes <input type="text" placeholder="..."> .</p>
                <button class="reveal-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('g1Ans').classList.toggle('show')">Reveal answers</button>
                <div class="answers" id="g1Ans">
                    <p><strong>1.</strong> was carried <em>(past simple passive)</em></p>
                    <p><strong>2.</strong> was guarded <em>(past simple passive)</em></p>
                    <p><strong>3.</strong> have been revived <em>(present perfect passive)</em></p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3>2. Fix the form</h3>
            <p>Each sentence uses the passive but the verb form is wrong. Write the corrected verb(s).</p>
            <div class="gap-fill">
                <p>1. Paper-making was <input type="text" placeholder="..."> (spread) west out of China. <em>(past participle needed)</em></p>
                <p>2. The cave temples of Dunhuang were <input type="text" placeholder="..."> (cut) into the cliffs. <em>(past participle needed)</em></p>
                <p>3. The desert could not <input type="text" placeholder="..."> (cross) without water. <em>(after a modal)</em></p>
                <button class="reveal-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('g2Ans').classList.toggle('show')">Reveal answers</button>
                <div class="answers" id="g2Ans">
                    <p><strong>1.</strong> spread <em>(spread–spread–spread; "was spread")</em></p>
                    <p><strong>2.</strong> cut <em>(cut–cut–cut; "were cut")</em></p>
                    <p><strong>3.</strong> be crossed <em>(modal + be + past participle)</em></p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3>3. Complete the passage</h3>
            <p>Fill each gap with the correct passive form of the verb in brackets. Read it aloud once you're done.</p>
            <div class="gap-fill">
                <p>The routes <input type="text" placeholder="..."> (call) the Silk Road only in modern times. Along them, silk and spices <input type="text" placeholder="..."> (trade) for gold and horses, and whole religions <input type="text" placeholder="..."> (spread) from land to land. Today the network <input type="text" placeholder="..."> (remember) as the first great meeting of East and West.</p>
                <button class="reveal-btn" onclick="document.getElementById('g3Ans').classList.toggle('show')">Reveal answers</button>
                <div class="answers" id="g3Ans">
                    <p>were called (past) · were traded (past) · were spread (past) · is remembered (present)</p>
                    <p><em>Notice how the passive keeps the focus on the routes and the goods — the things affected — rather than on any particular trader.</em></p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <p style="margin-top:14px; color:#666; font-style:italic;">Hold on to the passive — you'll need it in the Speak tab, where you'll describe what <em>was traded, was carried and was spread</em> along the roads.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- TAB 3: LISTEN -->
    <div class="tab-content">
        <div class="card">
            <h2>Hear it for real</h2>
            <p>You've read the story and you have the words — so now the real thing is within reach. There's a full podcast episode that tells this story in much greater depth, in natural, real-speed English. It's optional: the lesson stands on its own without it.</p>

