    <!-- TAB 4: SPEAK -->
    <div class="tab-content">
        <div class="card">
            <h2>Speak</h2>
            <p>This is the heart of the lesson — do all of it out loud. The timer is there to keep you going: fill the time with detail rather than escaping it.</p>

            <div class="note">
                <strong>Useful language:</strong> lean on the passive to describe the network — <em>silk was traded</em>, <em>ideas were carried</em>, <em>religions were spread</em>, <em>the routes have been revived</em>. For opinions, reach for: "What's striking is…", "You could argue that…", "It's no accident that…", "On one level… but on another…".
            </div>

            <div class="timer">
                <span>Time remaining: <strong><span id="timer-display">4:00</span></strong></span>
                <div class="timer-controls">
                    <button class="duration-btn" onclick="setDuration(this, 3)">3 min</button>
                    <button class="duration-btn active" onclick="setDuration(this, 4)">4 min</button>
                    <button class="duration-btn" onclick="setDuration(this, 5)">5 min</button>
                    <button class="timer-btn" onclick="startTimer()">Start</button>
                    <button class="timer-btn secondary" onclick="resetTimer()">Reset</button>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="speak-prompt">
                <span class="speak-label">Task A · Tell the whole story (4 minutes)</span>
                <p>Tell the story of the Silk Road in your own words, as if to someone who has never heard of it. Move through the arc: <em>how the routes opened under the Han and Zhang Qian → what was traded and what ideas and religions travelled → the age of the Mongols and Marco Polo → the plague and the sea routes that ended it → the long echoes today.</em></p>
                <p>Use the <strong>passive voice</strong> to describe the network (what <em>was traded, was carried, was spread</em>), and work in at least <strong>five key words</strong> (lattice, caravan, commodity, intermediary, oasis, monopoly, disseminate, confluence, plague, diplomacy).</p>
            </div>

            <div class="speak-prompt">
                <span class="speak-label">Task B · Connection and its costs (4 minutes)</span>
                <p>The Silk Road brought immense wealth and cultural richness — but the same routes carried a plague that killed millions. Is deeper connection between distant peoples always worth its dangers? Build an argument, using modern examples you know — global trade, the internet, travel and how it spreads disease — and decide where, if anywhere, you'd draw the line.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="speak-prompt">
                <span class="speak-label">Task C · A meeting of cultures of your own (4 minutes)</span>
                <p>Describe a place or a moment where different cultures met and exchanged something — food, music, words, technology. Lean on the grammar: what <em>was brought</em>, what <em>was borrowed</em>, what <em>was changed</em> when the two sides met.</p>
                <p style="margin-top:6px;"><strong>Stuck for an idea? Pick one of these and run with it:</strong></p>
