                    <li>A famous trading city or port — past or present — and what was exchanged there.</li>
                    <li>A modern "Silk Road": a route, a network or a technology that connects distant places today.</li>
                    <li>An invention that was carried from one part of the world to another and changed it.</li>
                    <li>A market or bazaar you have visited, and the goods and people that met there.</li>
                    <li>A religion, a language or an art form that was spread far from where it began.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="final-discussion">
                <h3>To finish — the bigger questions</h3>
                <ol>
                    <li>The Silk Road existed for over a thousand years before anyone gave it a name. Why do you think we feel the need to name and simplify vast, messy histories like this one?</li>
                    <li>Goods, faiths and inventions all travelled the same roads. Which did the most to shape the modern world — the trade, the religions, or the technologies?</li>
                    <li>China's "Belt and Road" deliberately echoes the ancient network. Is reviving an old name a genuine link to the past, or just a powerful story told for today's purposes?</li>
                    <li><strong>Before you go — from memory:</strong> give three passive sentences about the Silk Road (one past, one present perfect, one with a modal), and explain why the passive fits each one.</li>
                </ol>
            </div>
        </div>
